WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.789 --> 00:00:06.130
The horror genre is home to a lot of quality
flicks, as well as low-budget schlock, making

00:00:06.130 --> 00:00:10.410
it hard for a viewer to determine what's good
and what's a waste of time.

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:14.130
Fortunately, we've patched together a list
of those films that are worth the watch.

00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:18.500
Though these movies are all pretty different
in subject matter and tone, they're all worthwhile

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:21.570
horror films that have helped define the genre.

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:22.810
The Exorcist

00:00:22.810 --> 00:00:28.250
Even four decades after its release, 1973's
The Exorcist is still the possession thriller

00:00:28.250 --> 00:00:30.890
by which all others are judged, and for good
reason.

00:00:30.890 --> 00:00:35.720
Its ability to infuse a stewing sense of dread
alongside moments of body horror stands alone.

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:40.730
The grotesque tour de force made no apologies
for its use of profanity or gross body fluid

00:00:40.730 --> 00:00:43.530
spewing, and it's a lasting classic as a result.

00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:45.600
The Texas Chainsaw Massacre

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:49.780
Existing at the perfect intersection of art
and schlock, The Texas Chainsaw Massacre from

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:54.520
1974 is a tangibly dirty, grungy, and frightening
movie.

00:00:54.520 --> 00:00:58.770
Launching sequels and a remake franchise that
are by turns too loud and too trashy, the

00:00:58.770 --> 00:01:02.970
movie that started it all is an exercise in
unpleasantness.

00:01:02.970 --> 00:01:07.740
Its characters are largely unlikable, its
villains the perfect version of incomprehensible

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:08.750
hillbilly cruelty.

00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:13.080
"Look what your brother did to the door!"

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:17.490
The plot, essentially about a Texas road trip
that goes south fast, will remind you why

00:01:17.490 --> 00:01:22.010
they tell you not to ever talk to strangers,
pick up hitchhikers, or ask questions about

00:01:22.010 --> 00:01:25.729
what's going on in the rickety old house at
the edge of town.

00:01:25.729 --> 00:01:26.979
Invasion of the Body Snatchers

00:01:26.979 --> 00:01:32.030
The very definition of a time-worn tale in
cinema, the 1978 version of Invasion of the

00:01:32.030 --> 00:01:36.640
Body Snatchers is a story that's been remade
over and over again, which speaks to the strength

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:37.710
of its premise.

00:01:37.710 --> 00:01:42.770
The feeling of being helpless in an unrecognizable
society is something many might identify with,

00:01:42.770 --> 00:01:47.420
but the slow burn introduction of these alien
invaders is deeply disturbing.

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:50.979
Thrilling to the final frame, it's a story
that stays with you, anchored by an excellent

00:01:50.979 --> 00:01:55.150
cast of smart, unique characters that you
root for — making it hurt all the more when

00:01:55.150 --> 00:01:57.290
they fail and fall.

00:01:57.290 --> 00:01:58.290
Alien

00:01:58.290 --> 00:02:02.910
Ridley Scott's Alien from 1979 continues to
resonate so many years later because it has

00:02:02.910 --> 00:02:07.810
a lot of unique qualities — a female protagonist
who's just as strong and capable as anybody

00:02:07.810 --> 00:02:12.269
else, a profound sense of isolation, and an
enemy that cannot be reasoned with.

00:02:12.269 --> 00:02:16.049
Its characters are constantly under the gun,
tasked with identifying their threat while

00:02:16.049 --> 00:02:18.639
simultaneously struggling to survive against
it.

00:02:18.639 --> 00:02:22.659
Beyond the impressive intellectual framework,
there are also some shock and awe scenes that

00:02:22.659 --> 00:02:25.510
have since become iconic moments of cinema.

00:02:25.510 --> 00:02:26.510
The Shining

00:02:26.510 --> 00:02:31.219
With brilliant performances from its cast,
quotable lines, unmatched cinematography,

00:02:31.219 --> 00:02:36.659
and those awful twins, The Shining from 1980
is an absolute masterpiece.

00:02:36.659 --> 00:02:39.239
"Hereee's Johnny!"

00:02:39.239 --> 00:02:43.440
It worms its way into your head with beautiful
imagery and a hypnotic pace, showing the dissolution

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:47.390
of one man's sanity over the course of an
isolated winter at the Overlook Hotel.

00:02:47.390 --> 00:02:51.730
Stanley Kubrick's at his best here, with deliberate
choices made for each frame that's as visually

00:02:51.730 --> 00:02:55.010
stunning as it is packed with unforgettable
tension.

00:02:55.010 --> 00:02:56.010
The Thing

00:02:56.010 --> 00:03:00.530
John Carpenter is considered the Master of
Horror, and this may well be his best effort

00:03:00.530 --> 00:03:01.659
in the genre.

00:03:01.659 --> 00:03:05.650
Taking creature feature elements and combining
them with the who's-who paranoia of a Body

00:03:05.650 --> 00:03:10.969
Snatchers remake, The Thing from 1982 plants
its characters in isolated Antarctica and

00:03:10.969 --> 00:03:15.329
tasks them with surviving against an alien
lifeform that can assume their likenesses.

00:03:15.329 --> 00:03:19.029
It's a rich premise that combines subtle,
character-based interplay with full-blown

00:03:19.029 --> 00:03:22.930
body horror, like a human head scuttling around
with spider legs.

00:03:22.930 --> 00:03:27.109
With a cast to root for and a villain to fear,
The Thing is Exhibit A in the argument for

00:03:27.109 --> 00:03:32.090
John Carpenter's primacy among horror engineers,
a remake that runs laps around its source

00:03:32.090 --> 00:03:35.650
material and bests any modern attempts to
do better.

00:03:35.650 --> 00:03:37.120
Return of the Living Dead

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:41.430
An offshoot of George Romero's Living Dead
films, this spiritual cousin is easily one

00:03:41.430 --> 00:03:44.650
of the most goofily enjoyable zombie movies
ever.

00:03:44.650 --> 00:03:49.569
1985's Return of the Living Dead follows a
group of dirtbag ravers whose graveyard party

00:03:49.569 --> 00:03:53.330
gets upset by a zombie uprising caused by
acid rain.

00:03:53.330 --> 00:03:57.730
Return takes a lively approach to its material
that still feels fresh, even if the hilariously

00:03:57.730 --> 00:04:01.420
dated fashion of the characters decidedly
does not.

00:04:01.420 --> 00:04:03.900
"Why do you eat people?"

00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:06.300
"Not people, brains!"

00:04:07.820 --> 00:04:11.989
Being too self-serious about zombie material
is a trap that lots of movies fellows fall

00:04:11.989 --> 00:04:14.540
into, but Return sidesteps that problem with
aplomb.

00:04:14.540 --> 00:04:17.700
It's not a parody — just a great ride.

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:18.700
Jacob's Ladder

00:04:18.700 --> 00:04:23.690
This quiet character study from 1990 follows
Jacob Singer, a Vietnam veteran and postal

00:04:23.690 --> 00:04:28.500
service worker who slowly comes to believe
that either his sanity or reality itself is

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:29.900
falling apart.

00:04:29.900 --> 00:04:32.210
The film is notable for some standout sequences.

00:04:32.210 --> 00:04:37.010
Take, for example, Singer's long gurney ride
through a hellish hospital littered with body

00:04:37.010 --> 00:04:39.690
parts, blood, and inhuman monsters gone mad.

00:04:39.690 --> 00:04:43.750
The sequence has influenced horror media for
decades afterward, so even if you haven't

00:04:43.750 --> 00:04:46.750
seen this movie, you've definitely seen its
impact.

00:04:46.750 --> 00:04:51.130
In the decades since its release, it's developed
into a noted cult classic, lauded for its

00:04:51.130 --> 00:04:55.630
indelible imagery as much as the deep melancholy
at its core.

00:04:55.630 --> 00:04:56.630
Scream

00:04:56.630 --> 00:05:00.880
The first film ever to kick off the self-aware
horror trend is also still the best.

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:03.180
"Oh just some scary movie."

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:04.840
"You like scary movies?"

00:05:04.840 --> 00:05:05.840
"Uh huh."

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:08.000
"What's your favorite scary movie?"

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:11.810
Wes Craven — who was already horror royalty
thanks to The Hills Have Eyes, The People

00:05:11.810 --> 00:05:16.010
Under the Stairs, and the Nightmare on Elm
Street franchise — outdid himself with Scream

00:05:16.010 --> 00:05:17.660
in 1996.

00:05:17.660 --> 00:05:21.890
This funny, freaky slasher flick subverted
every trope in the book and roundly mocked

00:05:21.890 --> 00:05:25.980
the horror genre at large, while still scaring
the hell out of its audience.

00:05:25.980 --> 00:05:28.740
"Oh you wanna play psycho killer?"

00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:32.360
"Can I be the helpless victim?"

00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:34.140
The Blair Witch Project

00:05:34.140 --> 00:05:38.720
The found-footage project that started it
all, 1999's The Blair Witch Project launched

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:43.090
plenty of copycat projects that never got
anywhere close to the haunted, pine-scented

00:05:43.090 --> 00:05:44.500
quality of the original.

00:05:44.500 --> 00:05:48.590
The forest itself becomes the monster in this
ultra low-budget camcorder picture, where

00:05:48.590 --> 00:05:52.430
every tangle of twigs and leaves takes on
eerie suspicion.

00:05:52.430 --> 00:05:56.930
Following a group of hapless young filmmakers
as they circle the wilderness, lost and increasingly

00:05:56.930 --> 00:06:01.720
certain they're being hunted, the film is
a story about madness, isolation and distrust

00:06:01.720 --> 00:06:04.860
while still infusing a classic ghost story
in its bones.

00:06:04.860 --> 00:06:09.140
Its title antagonist is never truly seen and
maybe not even real, but that's part of the

00:06:09.140 --> 00:06:10.210
beauty of the film.

00:06:10.210 --> 00:06:14.930
The witch's absence offers an uncertainty
that gets to the heart of what horror is — fear,

00:06:14.930 --> 00:06:18.490
and a sense of helplessness, in the face of
the unknown.

00:06:18.490 --> 00:06:19.490
Audition

00:06:19.490 --> 00:06:23.830
Back in the days before OKCupid or eHarmony,
a widowed middle-aged businessman had to get

00:06:23.830 --> 00:06:27.500
creative when it came to finding a date…
or at least, that's the premise of Audition

00:06:27.500 --> 00:06:28.980
from 1999.

00:06:28.980 --> 00:06:33.650
This Japanese horror film is a very slow burn
with a scream-worthy finale, as the poor lovelorn

00:06:33.650 --> 00:06:37.950
protagonist realizes that the young lady he's
auditioned to be his new bride comes with

00:06:37.950 --> 00:06:40.860
a checkered past and some disturbing baggage.

00:06:40.860 --> 00:06:45.330
In addition to boasting stellar performances
and a creative, scary script, Audition has

00:06:45.330 --> 00:06:50.639
the added benefit of making your worst Tinder
date look like a great time by comparison.

00:06:50.639 --> 00:06:51.710
The Descent

00:06:51.710 --> 00:06:53.210
Claustrophobes be wary.

00:06:53.210 --> 00:06:57.590
If you've got a fear of being trapped in tight
spaces, The Descent from 2005 is one of the

00:06:57.590 --> 00:06:59.480
scariest movies you could watch.

00:06:59.480 --> 00:07:03.501
The unique story of a group of female cavers
who get lost in the uncharted depths, The

00:07:03.501 --> 00:07:07.440
Descent is a top-notch horror experience that
gets taken to the next level when the heroes

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:11.100
stop spelunking and have to start caving-in
monsters' skulls.

00:07:11.100 --> 00:07:15.139
They've got to fight for survival, all while
wading through massive lakes of blood.

00:07:15.139 --> 00:07:19.110
Be sure to catch the version with the original
ending, which was deemed too dark for U.S.

00:07:19.110 --> 00:07:22.120
audiences and cut from the theatrical release.

00:07:22.120 --> 00:07:23.120
The Mist

00:07:23.120 --> 00:07:27.090
A lot of Stephen King's horror novels have
been made into movies or TV shows, but only

00:07:27.090 --> 00:07:31.700
some of them have been successful at translating
the tension and terror of the source material

00:07:31.700 --> 00:07:32.700
to the screen.

00:07:32.700 --> 00:07:34.130
"I don't think you'd like it Henry."

00:07:34.130 --> 00:07:38.330
The Mist was of the few to get it right thanks
to the unsettling plot tweaks sprinkled in

00:07:38.330 --> 00:07:40.340
by writer-director Frank Darabont.

00:07:40.340 --> 00:07:45.780
The 2007 film tells the story of a New England
town beset by unspeakable, otherworldly horrors

00:07:45.780 --> 00:07:49.710
that descend under the cover of an impenetrable,
mysterious mist.

00:07:49.710 --> 00:07:53.310
It's a solid monster movie, but its scariest
moments are the ones that reveal the dark

00:07:53.310 --> 00:07:57.210
tribalism of human beings who believe they're
witnessing the end of the world, and it pulls

00:07:57.210 --> 00:07:59.010
no punches on that front.

00:07:59.010 --> 00:08:03.600
King even famously said that Darabont's gut-wrenching
twist ending for the film is the one he wishes

00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:05.510
he'd written himself.

00:08:05.510 --> 00:08:07.080
Paranormal Activity

00:08:07.080 --> 00:08:11.030
Found footage horror got a fresh face with
this 2007 movie, which proved that you don't

00:08:11.030 --> 00:08:15.540
need a gothic mansion or dilapidated cabin
in the woods to make a highly effective haunting

00:08:15.540 --> 00:08:16.540
flick.

00:08:16.540 --> 00:08:19.810
All the action of Paranormal Activity takes
place in the comfy, carpeted setting of a

00:08:19.810 --> 00:08:23.860
suburban townhome, as a pair of newlyweds
learn that they're not entirely alone in their

00:08:23.860 --> 00:08:24.860
house.

00:08:24.860 --> 00:08:28.450
Like its genre predecessor The Blair Witch
Project, Paranormal Activity is all about

00:08:28.450 --> 00:08:29.610
the slow burn.

00:08:29.610 --> 00:08:33.629
Even the most ordinary occurrences like a
door swinging shut or a hall light illuminating

00:08:33.629 --> 00:08:38.180
offscreen are imbued with foreboding, and
the teeny-tiny cast does a convincing job

00:08:38.180 --> 00:08:41.099
of making the "found footage" premise feel
real.

00:08:41.099 --> 00:08:42.099
Martyrs

00:08:42.099 --> 00:08:46.589
Profoundly disturbing to the point of being
unwatchable, 2008's Martyrs was one of the

00:08:46.589 --> 00:08:50.749
progenitors of the French "extreme horror"
movement, and earned that distinction in every

00:08:50.749 --> 00:08:51.850
single way.

00:08:51.850 --> 00:08:56.510
From the opening sequence, which depicts the
chillingly merciless murder of an entire family,

00:08:56.510 --> 00:08:58.410
the movie only gets harder to watch.

00:08:58.410 --> 00:09:02.910
It's a "curiosity killed the cat" parable
about revenge, mercy, and humanity's search

00:09:02.910 --> 00:09:06.130
for meaning, and it's unlike anything you've
ever seen.

00:09:06.130 --> 00:09:09.459
It challenges you to keep watching right up
to the cruel and bitter end.

00:09:09.459 --> 00:09:13.610
But watch the original, rather than the 2016
remake -- because that one's scary for a whole

00:09:13.610 --> 00:09:15.569
different series of reasons.

00:09:15.569 --> 00:09:17.160
Let the Right One In

00:09:17.160 --> 00:09:21.600
Not to be confused with Let Me In, the regrettable
American remake, Let the Right One In was

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:23.540
a sleepy Swedish surprise.

00:09:23.540 --> 00:09:28.100
In the 2008 pic, a bullied young boy named
Oskar makes friends with an unusual new neighbor

00:09:28.100 --> 00:09:31.610
who only comes out at night because, well,
she's a vampire.

00:09:31.610 --> 00:09:35.310
But Let the Right One In doesn't go the way
of Twilight or True Blood and try to get too

00:09:35.310 --> 00:09:37.970
campy about human-vampire relations.

00:09:37.970 --> 00:09:42.460
Instead, it's a smart piece of commentary
on the loneliness of the human condition — that

00:09:42.460 --> 00:09:46.579
is, when people aren't being drained of blood,
bursting into flames, or getting torn to shreds

00:09:46.579 --> 00:09:48.910
by a bunch of cute little kitties.

00:09:48.910 --> 00:09:50.220
You're Next

00:09:50.220 --> 00:09:54.399
Home invasion thrillers are a staple subgenre
within the horror-film family, and You're

00:09:54.399 --> 00:09:58.350
Next is a perfect specimen of this often-mishandled
storyline.

00:09:58.350 --> 00:10:02.709
With an opening scene that sets just the right
mood, a stylish execution, and a bad-ass final

00:10:02.709 --> 00:10:06.949
girl who you'll be rooting for from the get-go
— there's nothing about this 2011 movie

00:10:06.949 --> 00:10:08.480
that isn't utterly on point.

00:10:08.480 --> 00:10:11.160
Except, maybe, for the fact that there's no
sequel.

00:10:11.160 --> 00:10:12.339
Goodnight Mommy

00:10:12.339 --> 00:10:17.990
This Austrian feature from 2014 is a master
class in making the audience feel very, very

00:10:17.990 --> 00:10:18.990
bad.

00:10:18.990 --> 00:10:22.339
Two twins develop a syndrome that makes them
believe their mother, lost in depression after

00:10:22.339 --> 00:10:25.839
being disfigured in an accident, has been
replaced by an impostor.

00:10:25.839 --> 00:10:30.079
The story unfolds with trainwreck inevitability,
as their investigation turns from curious

00:10:30.079 --> 00:10:34.499
to cruel, playing with the allegiances of
the audience in an excruciating way.

00:10:34.499 --> 00:10:38.149
The story is riddled with blank spaces and
patches of silence where a lesser film would

00:10:38.149 --> 00:10:40.050
try to tell the audience too much.

00:10:40.050 --> 00:10:43.779
The amazing result is that viewers have to
do the legwork on figuring out what's really

00:10:43.779 --> 00:10:44.779
going on.

00:10:44.779 --> 00:10:49.139
And the film is not so much about jump surprises
as it is about the pure horror of watching

00:10:49.139 --> 00:10:53.019
innocent children do terrible, unspeakable
things.

00:10:53.019 --> 00:10:54.209
The Witch

00:10:54.209 --> 00:10:58.459
Not only is The Witch historically authentic
to an eerie degree, but its careful approach

00:10:58.459 --> 00:11:03.060
to introducing scary, supernatural elements
into the real-life religious anxieties of

00:11:03.060 --> 00:11:06.110
colonial New Englanders is intensely unsettling.

00:11:06.110 --> 00:11:09.959
And the climax of this 2015 film is an emotional
piece of hell.

00:11:09.959 --> 00:11:14.829
A lot hinges on the endings of horror movies,
and this one sticks the landing with confidence.

00:11:14.829 --> 00:11:19.420
It's an unsparing picture, the very meaning
of dreadful, and a modern classic.

00:11:19.420 --> 00:11:20.420
Get Out

00:11:20.420 --> 00:11:24.769
A meet-the-parents situation goes terribly
awry in Jordan Peele's directorial debut Get

00:11:24.769 --> 00:11:29.610
Out, which hit theaters in 2017 amid a wave
of well-deserved buzz.

00:11:29.610 --> 00:11:33.339
The movie follows interracial couple Rose
and Chris, who are visiting for a weekend

00:11:33.339 --> 00:11:37.310
at Rose's parents' estate — a classy place
where the polished veneer of white liberal

00:11:37.310 --> 00:11:42.079
tolerance turns out to be masking some deep,
dark, terrifying secrets about why there aren't

00:11:42.079 --> 00:11:43.740
more black folks in town.

00:11:43.740 --> 00:11:48.220
Get Out fully lives up to the hype, delivering
suspense and scares while also tackling complex

00:11:48.220 --> 00:11:50.869
and uncomfortable racial issues with incisive
wit.

00:11:50.869 --> 00:11:54.610
If it doesn't make you scream, it'll definitely
make you squirm.

00:11:54.610 --> 00:11:58.879
"Sink into the floor, sink."

00:11:58.879 --> 00:11:59.879
Thanks for watching!

00:11:59.879 --> 00:12:02.139
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:12:02.139 --> 00:12:04.619
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

